              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the vegetable oil in a large saucepan over medium-high heat. Add the chicken pieces and brown on all sides for 4 to 5 minutes. Remove and set aside."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Reduce the heat to medium. Add the grated ginger and garlic to the same pan. Fry for 1 minute until fragrant."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Return the chicken to the pan. Add 800ml of water, the soy sauce, and Worcestershire sauce. Add 2 diced medium potatoes and 2 diced carrots. Bring to a boil, then reduce to a steady simmer."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Skim any foam from the surface. Simmer for 20 minutes until the potatoes and carrots are tender when pierced with a knife."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Remove the pan from the heat. Break the Japanese curry roux block into pieces and add them to the pan. Stir until fully dissolved."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Return the pan to low heat. Simmer gently for 5 minutes, stirring often, until the sauce is thick and glossy. Taste and adjust seasoning."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Serve with steamed rice. A popular variation is"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "katsu-curry"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ": pour the sauce over a panko-fried pork or chicken cutlet laid on the rice."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "symptom": "Sauce is too thin after adding the roux block",
              "cause": "The roux has not had enough time to thicken the liquid.",
              "fix": "Simmer on low heat for at least 5 minutes after the roux dissolves, stirring constantly. The starch in the roux needs heat to activate. If still too thin after 8 minutes, simmer uncovered for longer."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Curry tastes bland",
              "cause": "The roux block alone may not be enough seasoning for the volume of water used.",
              "fix": "Add a splash more Worcestershire sauce and soy sauce, a pinch of salt, and a small piece of dark chocolate (5g) or a teaspoon of mango chutney. These are common Japanese home additions."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Potatoes have broken down into the sauce",
              "cause": "The potatoes were diced too small or simmered too long.",
              "fix": "Cut potatoes into 3cm chunks. Check them after 15 minutes. They should be just tender. Overcooked floury potatoes disintegrate into the sauce."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Japanese curry arrived via the British Navy in the Meiji era, adapted from Indian curry as filtered through British colonial cooking. It was adopted enthusiastically by the Japanese military as an easy, nutritious mass meal. By the mid-20th century it had become one of Japan's most popular home dishes. The ready-made roux block, introduced in the 1950s, made it fast enough for weeknight cooking. Japanese children often name it as their favourite food."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}